<document lang="en" type="article" theme-accent="teal">
  <title>Collaboration in Delta</title>

  <team>
    <member id="rodrigo" name="Rodrigo Ribeiro" kind="human" color="blue"/>
    <member id="claude" name="Claude" kind="agent" color="purple"/>
  </team>

  <floating>
    <title>Review</title>
    <review/>
  </floating>

  <abstract>
    A small paper written by a human and an agent together. Every mark the two left in
    it — comments, tasks, tracked changes, status — lives in this very file; the compiled
    page shows them in place, and <c>delta build --final</c> strips them all.
  </abstract>

  <section id="sec-setup">
    <title>Setup</title>

    Let $K \subset \mathbb{R}^n$ be compact and $f \colon K \to \mathbb{R}$ continuous.
    <comment id="c-domain" by="rodrigo" date="2026-09-10" status="resolved">
      Do we need $n \ge 1$ stated? <reply by="claude" date="2026-09-10">No: $n = 0$ is a point, and the statement is trivially true there.</reply>
    </comment>

    <lemma id="lem-image" status="verified" by="claude" verified-by="rodrigo">
      <title>Continuous image of a compact set</title>
      $f(K)$ is compact.
    </lemma>
    <proof of="lem-image" status="verified" by="claude" verified-by="rodrigo">
      Take an open cover of $f(K)$; its preimages cover $K$, extract a finite subcover.
    </proof>
  </section>

  <section id="sec-main">
    <title>The main result</title>

    <theorem id="thm-bounded" status="review" by="claude">
      <title>Extreme value theorem</title>
      $f$ is bounded and attains its maximum and minimum on $K$.
    </theorem>
    <comment on="thm-bounded" by="rodrigo" date="2026-09-12">
      Title says "extreme value" but we only prove boundedness below. Either prove the
      attainment or rename.
    </comment>

    <proof of="thm-bounded" status="sketch" by="claude">
      By <ref to="lem-image"/>, $f(K)$ is compact, hence closed and bounded. So there is
      $M$ with <change id="ch-sign" by="claude" date="2026-09-12" note="the inequality was reversed"><old>$|f(x)| > M$</old><new>$|f(x)| \le M$</new></change>
      for every $x \in K$.<comment id="c-attain" by="claude" date="2026-09-12">
        Attainment: $\sup f(K) \in f(K)$ because $f(K)$ is closed. I have not written this
        step out yet.
      </comment>
      <change by="rodrigo" date="2026-09-13"><old>This completes the proof.</old></change>
    </proof>

    <todo id="t-attain" for="claude" by="rodrigo" status="open" priority="high" due="2026-09-20">
      Write out the attainment step in the proof of <ref to="thm-bounded"/> (closedness of $f(K)$).
    </todo>
    <todo id="t-title" for="rodrigo" status="doing">
      Decide between renaming <ref to="thm-bounded"/> and keeping the full statement.
    </todo>
    <todo id="t-lemma" for="claude" status="done">
      Prove <ref to="lem-image"/>.
    </todo>

    <change id="ch-corollary" by="claude" date="2026-09-13" note="a corollary the applications section will cite">
      <new>
        <corollary id="cor-sup">
          $\sup_K f$ and $\inf_K f$ are finite.
        </corollary>
      </new>
    </change>
  </section>

  <section id="sec-applications" status="draft" by="claude">
    <title>Applications</title>

    <draft by="claude" note="only a direction so far; the examples are missing">
      The theorem gives a uniform bound for any continuous function on a closed interval,
      which is the basic ingredient of Riemann integrability.
    </draft>
  </section>

  <section id="sec-review" numbered="false">
    <title>Review panel</title>
    The same panel the floating button opens, inline:
    <review/>
  </section>
